\version "2.24.0"
% c378 (expression; Adler ch. 7, adler1 PDF 186 Ex. VII-4 (Brahms 4/iv flute: a hairpin on each rising half-figure) and PDF 211 Ex. VII-93 (Tchaikovsky 6/i bassoon: every figure its own hairpin)): 102 and 106, the rising half of the wind scale figure (Fl 1, Ob 1, Cl 1) swells into its accented top note, as the same figure does at 44, 46 (c278) and 89-90 (c377); it now leads into the strings' f head at 103 and the lament at 107. Levels unchanged (mf); Fl 2, Ob 2, Cl 2 rest, so the shared staves get no unequal marks.
% c129 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 102: discords of the seventh or second go to instruments of different colour; PDF 108: concords to the horns, discords to the bassoons): 130-131 Hn 4 A3 (was C4 a second under Hn 3's D4); the seventh C4 stays in Bn 1 (and the viola line), so the horns hold D-F#-A.
% c172 (harmony; A&S Unit 27, PDF 465: a chord 7th 'promotes progression to a new chord, one that contains the tone of resolution'; PDF 466: unresolved 7ths 'occur most infrequently'): 115 beat 2 Vln II B flat4/G5 (was D5/G5), so the 7th C5 of the D7 pivot at 114 falls to B flat4 in its own part instead of rising to D5.
% c172 (A&S Unit 27, PDF 469 sec. 7 and Unit 21 sec. 12: a dissonance is weak when its tone of resolution sounds at the same time in a voice other than the bass; PDF 470: wide spacing is what keeps such a sonority clear): 117 beat 2 Vln II E flat5 alone (was E flat5/G5): its G5 sounded the solo's resolution a seventh above the appoggiatura A4; the full E flat/G chord comes on beat 4, after A4-G4.
% c213 (form; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 130: a prolonged crescendo adds strings, then wood-wind, then brass; PDF 127: the second group enters p and reaches its crescendo faster; Dvorak 9/iv 1-10: strings, then winds f <, then the ff tutti):
%   108-109 Ob 1 and Cl 1 double Vln II's suspension line G5-F5-E5 p < into the climax at 110, so the wood-wind join the strings' crescendo before the brass enter ff at 110. No pitch class added; bass unchanged.
% c304 (orchestration; Adler ch. 15, adler2 PDF 151: Mozart keeps the oboes off the passage's low last note, where they 'would have added a raucous quality', so 'every note in this passage [is] played effectively by every instrument'):
%   102 beat 4 Ob 1 rests; the octave drop's C4 stays in Cl 1 (chalumeau) and the C5 in Fl 1. 106 (Ob 1 F4) unchanged.
% c372 (harmony; A&S Unit 10, PDF 154 sec. 6 and Ex. 10-6a (PDF 155, "never!"): the cadential 6/4 must stand on the strong beat, with no weak-to-strong bass repetition; PDF 160 point 3; Unit 9, PDF 135, 139 sec. 6, 151 point 1: an intermediate harmony leads to V and prepares its 7th as a common tone): 129 beats 3-4 were a 6/4 (bass D under the tune's G) on the weak half resolving to V7 on the downbeat of 130. They are now IV6 (C-E-G over E; Vc E3, Cb E2, Bn 1 C4 tied into 130 as the prepared seventh, Hn 3 G4, Hn 4 E3, violas rest on beats 3-4 and enter fresh on the F1 head at 130 (any viola note there moved in fifths or octaves with the tune or the bass; C4-D4 gave fifths with the tune's G-A), harp E3-G3-C4-E4, same pedals). Bass B-E-D under the tune's G-A moves in contrary motion (a C bass would give outer fifths C-G to D-A). Tune, hand-over, rhythm and dynamics unchanged.
% DEVELOPMENT, bars 99-134.
% c033 (form; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 125-126, 128, 130, 136): 123-130 theme F2 in G major is no longer a harp solo (the scherzo's development has the harp melody, scherzo 73-80);
% it is passed upward Vc (123) -> Vla (124) -> Cl 1 (126) -> Vln I + Fl 1 (128), each entry on the previous part's last note, with a crescendo by addition into the f cadence at 131; the harp keeps the arpeggios.
% c017: 103-105 the violins enter with motive x half a bar after the low strings' head (stretto), 99-102 keep the two ideas apart first (Adler II PDF 177, 181).
% 99-106: call and response between strings (theme F1's head, F minor 99, B flat minor 103) and woodwinds (motive x, 101, 105).
% 107-110: lament bass F-E-E flat-D-D flat-C (i - V6 - v6 - IV6 - iv6 - V) with 7-6 suspensions in the violins (A&S Unit 31, PDF 551-583).
% 111-114: cadence in F minor; the bass rises F - F sharp (D7, V of G). 115-122: G minor, bassoon 1 solo on theme F2 over pizzicato strings
% (movement I gives the bassoon only short solos, cyclic-notes critic 54). 123-130: G major, theme F2 in the harp alone (its first melodic
% line in the symphony, cyclic-notes 'Still unused'). 131-134: cadence in G (D7-G, f): the development holds one key with its own cadence.

% c037 (texture; Adler ch. 16, PDF 202, 217, 228-231): 123-124 the violas rest until they take the theme on beat 4 of 124 (their held B3-C4 lay on and above the cellos' entry in the same colour);
% 124 bass F sharp (V6/5; harp arpeggio from F sharp), so 123-125 descend G-F sharp-E as 115-117 do in minor; 125-130 bassoon 1 counter-line under the violas' and clarinet's entries
% (the cellos keep the bass); 129-130 horns 3-4 repeated quarters p cresc. into their f at 131 (Beethoven 6/i 304-312: the crescendo gets a new rhythmic layer).
% c034 (orchestration; Adler ch. 15, PDF 154, 156): 123-126 Vln II rest (its held G4 pp lay on and above the violas' F2 G4-F sharp4-E4-D4 in the same colour); it re-enters arco pp at 127 under Cl 1.
% c032 (harmony; A&S Unit 17, PDF 276-277: falling-third root progressions need 10ths or 5-6 above the bass, not 5ths): 111 Ob 1 holds F5 and Cl 1 rises C5-D flat5 (were F-D flat, C-A flat: 8ves and 5ths with the bass F-D flat); 127 Vln II holds G4 (ii7; was E4: G-E over C-A, 5ths with the bass).
% c038 (expression; Casanelles ch. 4, PDF 148, 155, 152; ch. 6, PDF 290): 116-118 Bn 1 swell to and from the solo's peak G4, 122 diminuendo into the cellos' entry; harp pp under the p hand-over (123) and crescendo with the tutti 129-130; hairpins at 134 end on the rest (the target is the pp at 135).
% c042 (harmony; A&S Unit 22, PDF 375, 377, 379): F2's second and fourth bars change chord on beat 3, as at 52, 54, 68, 70: 116 and 124 V6/5 -> i/I (bass F sharp -> G; was V6/5 held under the tune's
% tonic notes, with the bass's d5 F sharp-C going to the fifth E flat-B flat / E-B and the leading tone falling), 118 and 126 iv/IV -> i6/I6 (bass C -> B flat/B; the tune's D was an unresolved 9th over C).
% 126 Bn 1 D4 (was E3: a 4th over the new B; G3 would make fifths with the violas' D4-E4 into 127); 129 Bn 1 B3 (was G3: its C4 at 128, the 7th of D7 doubled with the violas, leapt away instead of falling to B).
% c058 (expression; Sebesky ch. 6, PDF 217-218 Ex. 284: the drop in level comes with the change of colour): 114 the D7 pivot after the ff cadence and half-bar rest starts p (was ff dim. to pp in 1.4 s).
% c067 (Sebesky ch. 3, PDF 155: a flute on the lead violin line is marked a level below the strings): 129 Fl 1 mp < (Vln I mf <), both f at 131.
% c075 (MOLA Example 3, PDF 10): entries after rests carry their dynamic: 103 brass f (as at 99), 105 winds mf (as at 101; Cl 1's mf hidden in the score like its 101 mark, printed in its part)
% c131 (melody; Adler ch. 7, PDF 226, Ex. VII-89 (image): the bassoon's "thin, but intense" band runs from about C4 to B flat 4, "thin and often pinched" only from B4; c041 read A4 as the pinched edge): 117 Bn 1 restores F2's third bar, A4-G4-F4 (was G4 dotted half-F4), the leap of a fifth to the appoggiatura that 53, 69, 125 and 173 have; peak mp over the pp pizzicato (PDF 226: accompaniment marked below a high solo).
% c145 (Adler ch. 18, PDF 295: all instruments marked in all systems; PDF 301, Ex. XVIII-10a): 105 "1." for Fl 1 and Ob 1 (score only); after the page turn the shared staves showed one line with no label (last label at 101, page 5).
% c188 (Adler ch. 10, PDF 309, 321: slurred notes on one breath, separate notes tongued): 111-112 the winds and brass that double the violins' slurred G-A flat-F and 4-3 F-E (Fl 1-2, Ob 1, Tpt 1, Hn 2, Hn 4; Ob 2 and Tpt 2 D flat-C, Tpt 2 tied into 112) take the violins' slurs, so the suspension resolves legato in every part.
% c212 (harmony; A&S Unit 17, PDF 276-277: falling-third root progressions need 10ths or 5-6 above the bass; PDF 267: repeated patterns give 5ths and octaves unless the upper voices are led against the bass):
% 112 Cl 1 ties D flat 5 over the barline and falls D flat 5 - C5 with Ob 2 and Tpt 2 (was B flat 4 - G4: its D flat 5 (111.3) -> B flat 4 (112.1) moved in octaves with the bass D flat -> B flat, the falling third
% after the one c032 corrected at 111). D flat is the fifth of the half-diminished ii6/5 (G - B flat - D flat - F over B flat), C the fifth of V7; Cl 1 then falls C5 - A flat 4 into 113.
devFluteOne = { R1*2 | r4 c'''8(\mf bes'' aes'' g'' f'' e'' | f''8\< g'' aes'' bes'' c'''4->)\! c''4-> | R1*2 | r4 f'''8(\mf-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." es''' des''' c''' bes'' a'' | \once \offset positions #'(-0.25 . 0.25) Beam bes''8\< c''' des''' es''' f'''4->)\! f''4-> | R1*3 | g'''1\ff~ | g'''4( aes'''2 f'''4) | f'''2~( f'''4 e'''4) | f'''2-> r2 | R1 | R1*8 | R1*6 | b''2(\mp\< g''2 | a''1) | a''1\f | g''4-> r4 r2 | R1*2 | }
devFluteTwo = { R1*11 | g''1\ff~ | g''4( aes''2 f''4) | f''2~( f''4 e''4) | f''2-> r2 | R1*21 | }
devOboeTwo = { R1*11 | e''1\ff | \once \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #0 f''1 | des''2( c''2) | c''2-> r2 | R1*21 | }
% c054 (orchestration; Adler ch. 8, PDF 255, 267-268, 250): 110-113 the melody G5-A flat5-F5-E5-F5 (Vln I) is doubled by Fl 1 an octave above, Fl 2 and Ob 1 at pitch;
% Ob 2 takes Ob 1's old line; trumpets, horns 1-2, trombones and tuba f under the ff strings and winds (Dvorak, Carnival 25-31: brass kept down so strings and winds come through).
% c092 (harmony; A&S Unit 5, PDF 76: the chord 7th is never doubled; PDF 83: parallel octaves): 131 Ob 1 F sharp5-D5-B4 (was F sharp5-C5-B4: its C5-B4 doubled Hn 4's C4-B3, the 7th resolving in octaves); 132 Cl 1 keeps D5 (was D5-G4 in octaves with the bass D-G).
devOboeOne = { R1*2 | r4 c''8(\mf bes' aes' g' f' e' | f'8\< g' aes' bes' c''4->)\! r4 | R1*2 | r4 f''8(\mf-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." es'' des'' c'' bes' a' | bes'8\< c'' des'' es'' f''4->)\! f'4-> | R1 | g''2~\p\<-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." g''4( f''4~ | f''2. e''4) | g''1\ff~ | \once \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #1.3 g''4( aes''2 f''4) | f''2~( f''4 e''4) | f''2-> r2 | R1 | R1*8 | R1*8 | fis''2\f d''2 | b'4-> r4 r2 | R1*2 | }
% c075 (MOLA PDF 8 and Example 7, PDF 12: an extracted part must not lose or float its marks): the hidden mf at 101 (printed once on the shared staff) and the "1." at 126 are score-only (tag 'score); the Cl 1 part prints its own mf
% c205 (part-combining labels): 126 printed "1." twice, the combiner's solo label (since \partCombineAutomatic at 111) and the c035/c075 score-only markup; the markup is removed. The Cl 1 part had no "1." there (tag 'score).
% c234 (orchestration; Adler ch. 8, adler1 PDF 255: 'most of the great masters try to alter something, no matter how slightly, the third time they use the
% same orchestration; this is often true of any sequential material'; PDF 250-251, Ex. VIII-17: winds a 2 in octaves, the organ's 8'-4'-2' coupling, 'transparent
% but forceful'; PDF 237, Ex. VIII-5: flute, clarinet and bassoon in a three-octave spread): the scale figure's third appearance in the sequence 99-106 (winds 101,
% violins 103, winds 105) repeated 101's scoring exactly (Fl 1 an octave over Ob 1 + Cl 1 in unison). At 105 Cl 1 plays it an octave below Ob 1 (F4-A3-F4, F3),
% so the answer before the lament spans three octaves (Fl 1 F6, Ob 1 F5, Cl 1 F4) over the bass clarinet's A2; 101 keeps the oboe-clarinet unison (PDF 219-220, Schubert 8).
% c239 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. IV, PDF 132-133: a florid line should not run against a held note of the same colour in the same register; between wood-wind and brass the harmonic basis
% stays an octave away from the melodic design and is softer; PDF 116: a phrase in the brass takes its harmony in the wood-wind): 103-104 the held F that binds the F1 head to the violins'
% answer moves from Hn 1-2 (F4, inside Hn 3-4's head B flat4-F4-G flat4-A flat4-G flat4-F4-E flat4-D flat4) to Cl 1 (F5, p), an octave above; Hn 1-2 rest 103-104 and re-enter p at 105.
% c323 (form; A&S Unit 18, PDF 297-299, Ex. 18-12: the passing minor dominant leads i to the iv6 of a Phrygian cadence by a stepwise bass 1-b7-b6-5, and 'a 7-6 suspension into the 6th of IV6 ... such suspensions are almost invariably found'): 121-122 Cl 1 pp holds D6 from 121.2 (a 6th over the passing F, the outer voices in 6ths) across the bass E flat (a 7th) and resolves it to C sharp6 on beat 4, the augmented 6th of the Italian sixth, which opens to D6 over the bass D on 122.1; Vln I's solo desks take D6 from it on 122.2. In the pizzicato texture the E flat (121.3) and the C sharp (121.4) were never heard together, so the development's only half cadence in G minor had no sounding augmented sixth and no suspension. Solo, pizzicato, bass and 123-130 unchanged.
devClOne = { R1*2 | r4 c''8(\mf bes' aes' g' f' e' | f'8\< g' aes' bes' c''4->)\! c'4-> | f''1~\p | f''1 | r4 f'8(\mf es' des' c' bes a | bes8\< c' des' es' f'4->)\! f4-> | \partCombineAutomatic R1 | g''2~\p\<-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." g''4( f''4~ | f''2. e''4) | c''1\ff | c''2 des''2~ | des''2( c''2) | aes'2-> r2 | R1 | R1*6 | r4 d'''2(\pp cis'''4 | d'''4.) r8 r2 | R1*3 | r2 d''2(\p\< | c''2.\mf e''4 | d''4 c''4 b'4 a'4) | R1*2 | d''1\f | d''4-> r4 r2 | R1*2 | }
% c178 (expression; Adler ch. 7, adler1 PDF 210-211: the clarinet plays every dynamic from pp to ff in every register, so nothing in the instrument asks for mf): 101-102 and 105-106 Cl 2 rests. It doubled Hn 2 at the unison (E4-F4-E4, A4-B flat4-A4) at mf, a hidden mark taken from Cl 1's scale figure on the shared staff, while Hn 1-2 and Bn 1 hold the same chord p; the pad is now one dynamic (p), and Cl 1 plays the figure as "1." on the staff.
% c187 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 76: a wind in unison with a string group increases its resonance and amplifies its tone; PDF 79, three groups for loud passages):
% 110-113 Cl 2 doubles the violas' scale figure (G3-F3 ... A flat 3) with Bn 1, so the figure is not covered by the ff brass chord in the same register.
devClTwo = { R1*8 | R1*3 | g4\ff c'8( bes aes g f g) | f8( g aes bes aes2) | g2 c'8( bes aes g) | aes2-> r2 | R1 | R1*8 | R1*8 | R1*4 | }
% c035 (Gould PDF 60, 65): 111-113 Bn 1 crosses below Bn 2; written apart (two stems) instead of as single-stemmed chords that hid the crossing.
% c062 (A&S Unit 21, PDF 352; Unit 28-29 leading-tone rule): 111 and 113 Bn 1 takes the leading tone E3 up to F3 (was E3-C3); 112 Bn 1 holds F3 to beat 4 with the horns and flutes so E is not sounded under the 4-3 suspension.
% c063 (form/accompaniment; Adler ch. 17, PDF 263-264, 267: the solo colour enters fresh, with no orchestral instrument of its own colour just before it): 114 Bn 2 rests (its held D3 p anticipated the bassoon's D4 entry); the strings alone carry the D7 pivot.
% c075: "1." at 125 score-only (tag 'score); the Bn 1 part keeps "dolce"
% c135 (MOLA PDF 8: proofread the vertical lines as well as the horizontal): 116 Bn 1's first A after its A flat at 113 and the key change at 115 gets the courtesy natural that Vln I's A5 has at 117 (c132), so the two read alike.
% c137 (texture; Adler ch. 16, PDF 229: two colours doubling at pitch swallow each other): 126 Bn 1's counter C4-G3 (was C4-D4, a half note in unison with the violas' melody D4); it no longer meets the melody, and avoids the octaves C-B with the cellos.
% c181 (A&S Unit 20, PDF 330-331 section 4 and PDF 343 point 8; the c-comment above on 126 already warned that G3 makes fifths): 126 Bn 1 D4-A3 on beats 3-4 (was G3 half),
% A3 taken early and tied into 127, so its counter no longer moves G3/D4 -> A3/E4 in fifths with the violas' F2: the fifth A3/E4 is reached by oblique motion (A&S PDF 342, Ex. 20-27).
% Not B3 (C4-B3 would be octaves with the bass C-B, as c137 noted); D4 meets the violas' D4 for one quarter only (c137 removed a half-note unison). A rest on beat 3 adds a page.
% c187 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 79, Ex. 91, Tsar Saltan [92]: Violas + Fag. / 'Cellos + Fag. / D. basses): 99-100 Bn 1 doubles the violas' F1 head at F4, as Bn 2 doubles the cellos at F3.
% c327 (texture; Adler ch. 17, adler2 PDF 283 Ex. XVII-22, Fidelio: 'often a bass part is doubled several octaves higher'; PDF 288 Ex. XVII-24, Haydn: the flute doubles the tenor two octaves up): 107-109 Bn 1 doubles the lament bass F-E-E flat-D-D flat-C at F4-C4, two octaves above the contrabassoon and basses, p <, so the chromatic descent is heard as a line in the tenor (was Bn 1 tacet, Bn 2 alone at F3). Bass, strings, suspensions unchanged; only a doubling added (as c213).
devBsnOne = { \partCombineApart f'2->(\f c'4. des'8 | es'4-.) des'8( c'8 bes4-.) aes4-. | r4 e2.\p \partCombineAutomatic | e2 e2 | R1*2 | r4 a2.\p | a2 a2 | \partCombineApart f'2\p\< e'2 | es'2 d'2 | des'2 c'2 | e4\ff c'8( bes aes g f g) | f2 f2 | f2 c'8( bes aes g) | aes2-> r2 | \partCombineAutomatic R1 | d'2.(\p^\markup \italic "solo, espress." bes4 | a!4 g4\< bes4 d'4 | a'2\mp g'4 f'4\> | es'2 d'2\p) \breathe | c'2.( es'4 | d'4 c'4 bes4 a4 | bes2 g2 | a1\>) | R1*2\! | e2(\p-\tag #'score ^\markup { \bold "1." \italic "dolce" } -\tag #'part ^\markup \italic "dolce" fis4 g4 | c'2 d'4 a4~ | a2 b4 c'4 | a2 c'2 | b2\< c'2~ | c'1) | c'1\f | b4-> r4 r2 | R1*2 | }
devBsnTwo = { f2->(\f c4. des8 | es4-.) des8( c8 bes,4-.) aes,4-. | f,4-.\sf r4 r2 | R1 | bes2->(\f f4. ges8 | aes4-.) ges8( f8 es4-.) des4-. | bes,4-.\sf r4 r2 | R1 | f2\p\< e2 | es2 d2 | des2 c2 | c1\ff | c2 des2 | bes,2 c2 | c2-> r2 | R1 | R1*8 | R1*8 | d2\f d2 | g,4-> r4 r2 | R1*2 | }
% c194 (orchestration; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. III, PDF 101: 'the third trombone and the tuba usually form the bass in octaves'; PDF 112: 'in every chord the parts in octaves
% strengthen one another, the harmonic sounds in the lowest register coinciding with and supporting those in the highest'): the climax 110-113 had no 16-foot octave: tuba,
% contrabassoon, basses, bass trombone and (at 113) cellos all on C2/F2 under Fl 1 and Vln I at G6-F6. 110 Cbsn C1; 111 beat 1 Cbsn and tuba F1 under the bass trombone's F2;
% 113 tuba, Cbsn and basses F1 (basses' lowest string E1), so the F-minor goal chord has F1-F2 under the cellos' and bass trombone's F2. 112 and 111 beats 3-4 unchanged.
% c234 (orchestration; Adler ch. 8, adler1 PDF 257 and Ex. VIII-25 (image, PDF 257): in a first inversion the bass (third) is doubled toward the bottom, with
% the bass clarinet under the bassoons, so the chord keeps its open quality; PDF 252-253: when the choirs answer each other each should be complete in itself;
% PDF 260, Ex. VIII-32: the bass clarinet's entry is a fresh colour inside a wind passage): 101-102 and 105-106, where the strings rest and the woodwinds answer
% the strings' F1 head with the scale figure over a p pad (Hn 1-2, Bn 1), the pad's bass E3 (C7 over E) and A3 (F7 over A) was the lowest sound, a tenth or
% more above the strings' F2/B flat 1 in the bars before, and its third was doubled upward (Hn 2 E4, A4). The bass clarinet doubles Bn 1 an octave below, p,
% tied (E2, A2), so the wind answer has the depth of the string call. No pitch class added; the bass clarinet's first notes since 34.
% c255 (MOLA PDF 5): the bass clarinet's p entry at 101 follows 66 bars of rest (35-100); part-only cue of Bn 1 (F1's head, with the violas) at 99-100
devBassCl = { \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "bsnOne" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Bn. 1" } } | r4 e,2.~\p | e,1 | R1*2 | r4 a,2.~\p | a,1 | R1*28 | }
devCbsn = { R1*8 | f,2\p\< e,2 | es,2 d,2 | des,2 c,2 | c,,1\ff | f,,2 des,2 | bes,,2 c,2 | f,,2-> r2 | R1 | R1*8 | R1*8 | R1*4 | }
% c151 (melody; A&S Unit 21, PDF 350 sec. 4: a line is brought into prominence; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 113 (printed 95): 'the splendid effect of horns doubled by divided violas or cellos'): 130-132 Hn 2 doubles the violas' F1 head over D7 (D4-A3-B3 | C4-B3-A3-G3-F sharp 3 | G3; was rest, then D4 at 131 and G4 at 132), so the movement's first theme is heard against F2's half cadence; Hn 1 marked mf < at 130 with it (shared staff). The 123-130 hand-over of F2 is unchanged.
% c157 (texture; Sebesky ch. 1, PDF 12-13, Ex. 1 and PDF 13 focus): 130 Hn 3 holds D4 (was repeated D4 quarters on the beats of F1's head, which starts on the same D4 in Hn 2-4 and the violas; Hn 4 left that layer at 130 for the head); 131 Bn 1 holds the seventh C4 and resolves it to B3 at 132 (was C4-A3, the A3 a second from the head's G3-F sharp 3 in the same register).
devHnOne = { R1*2 | r4 g'2.\p | aes'2 g'2 | R1*2 | r4 c''2.\p | des''2 c''2 | R1*3 | g'1\ff | aes'2 aes'2 | bes'2 g'2 | aes'2-> r2 | R1*13 | e'1\pp | fis'2( a'2) | g'1\< | fis'1\mf\< | a'1\f | g'4-> r4 r2 | R1*2 | }
devHnTwo = { R1*2 | r4 e'2.\p | f'2 e'2 | R1*2 | r4 a'2.\p | bes'2 a'2 | R1*3 | e'1\ff | f'2 f'2 | f'2~( f'4 e'4) | f'2-> r2 | R1*16 | d'2->(\mf\< a4. b8 | c'4-.\f) b8( a8 g4-.) fis4-. | g4-> r4 r2 | R1*2 | }
% c064 (orchestration; Adler ch. 11, PDF 15: above mf two horns in unison equal one trumpet or trombone; PDF 44, Bartok: the same rule in a loud brass passage):
% 110-113 Hn 3 doubles Hn 1 (G4-A flat4-B flat4/G4-A flat4) and Hn 4 doubles Hn 2 (E4-F4-F4/E4-F4), so the horns' third and fifth of V7 of F weigh as much as the single trumpets' and trombones' notes in the f brass chord.
% c039 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 130: a diminuendo removes brass, then wood-wind, then strings): Hn 3-4 leave at 133, the strings finish the diminuendo into 135.
% c288 (expression; Adler ch. 10, adler1 PDF 304-305, 309-310, 319-320: every diminuendo in the brass examples starts from a stated level and lands on one (Ex. X-11 p > pp, X-25 f > p, X-40 > p, X-44 ff > p, X-48 mf > pp); MOLA PDF 8: restate the dynamic after rests): 133-134 the V4/2 that closes the development fades from f to p, printed at 134 in the strings, before the retransition's pp at 135; Hn 3-4 restate f at 133 after their rest (the hairpin ran from an unstated level to nothing). Notes unchanged.
% c397 (Adler part 2 ch. 11, Ex. XI-24, PDF 45-47: each brass entry of the fugato subject is marked 'f, ben marc.' so it projects over the voices that go on): 103 Hn 3-4 and Tbn 1, whose
%   answer of F1's head is overlapped by the violins' scale figure (c017), are marked "ben marc." as at 37 (c297). Text only; notes and dynamics unchanged.
