% c075 (MOLA PDF 5: harp pedalling is left to the performer and not printed in the part): the diagram is score-only (tag 'score)
% c174 (orchestration; Casanelles ch. 6, PDF 286-287: sounds in the same frequency range mask each other; PDF 275-276: the attack transient is the plucked note's short inharmonic part; Adler ch. 4, PDF 113: the harp's lower two octaves are 'somber, dark'): 123 harp p (was pp): its G2-A3 broken chords share the octave with the cellos' bass, Bn 1's counter-line (125-130) and the basses' G2,
% so at pp only the attacks came through; at p, the cellos' level, it is the G-major passage's audible colour. Notes, register and pedals unchanged (c116).
% c346 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 46: harp chords are broken unless the composer writes non arpeggiato): 132 "non arpegg." on the solid ff G chord
devHarpL = { R1*8 | R1*8 | R1*4 | \tag #'score { R1*4 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "bsnOne" #UP { R1*4^\markup \tiny "Bn. 1" } } | \stemUp g,8\p-\tag #'score ^\markup \small \harp-pedal #"---|-v--" b,8 d8 g8 g,8 b,8 d8 g8 | fis,8 a,8 c8 d8 g,8 b,8 d8 g8 | g,8 b,8 e8 g8 g,8 b,8 e8 g8 | g,8 c8 e8 g8 g,8 b,8 d8 g8 | a,8 c8 e8 a8 a,8 c8 e8 a8 | a,8 d8 fis8 a8 a,8 d8 fis8 a8 | b,8\< d8 g8 b8 e8 g8 c'8 e'8 | a,8 c8 d8 fis8 a,8 c8 d8 fis8 | \stemNeutral r1\!^\markup \italic "étouffez" | <g, d b>4\ff->^\markup \small \italic "non arpegg." \laissezVibrer r4 r2 | R1 | r2 r2-\tweak self-alignment-X #CENTER ^\markup \italic "étouffez" | }  % c206 (Adler PDF 117, sons étouffés; as at 82 and 243): damp the low D7 arpeggio as the G-major cadence arrives at 131
% c059 (holistic, Tchaikovsky 4/iv; Adler part 1 PDF 152 items 2 and 6, PDF 154, PDF 267): 110-113 the violins take the climax melody in octaves, Vln I G6-A flat6-F6-E6-F6 with Fl 1,
% Vln II G5-A flat5-F5-E5-F5 with Fl 2 and Ob 1 (Vln II's former C5-D flat5 line stays in Ob 2, Cl 1 and Tpt 2). Pitches, harmony and dynamics unchanged.
% c062 (harmony; A&S Unit 29, PDF 503, 505, 510-511): 109 the lament bass's D flat carries a German sixth D flat-F-A flat-B natural (Vln I B flat-B-C rises against the falling bass, Vla A flat), which opens to the octave C and the cadential 6/4 (Vla A flat held, Vln II F-E) before the climactic V7 at 110.
% c063 (form; Adler II PDF 257-258: dialogue, a phrase answered at once in another colour "aids in a clearer exposition of the form"; PDF 274, Beethoven VC iii: the
% bassoon's line set against a contrasting activity, the answer kept two octaves above the woodwind solo): 117 and 122 Vln I, arco p against Vln II's pizz., answers the bassoon's
% held notes with the development's scale figure (winds at 101, 105), so the call of 99-106 carries into the lyrical centre; 117 descends F6-A flat5 to G5 at 118 (sixths and fourths over the solo's G4-F4-E flat4, no fifths); 122 descends D6-F sharp5 with B natural and lands on
% G5 at 123 with the turn to G major. Vln I then rests until its hand-over entry at 128.
% c085 (Adler ch. 2, PDF 37-38: a slur is one bow; an accented note after a run takes a new bow): 103-104 the violins' run closes its slur on E flat, and the accented F is a separate stroke, as the scale figure is bowed at 3-4, 87-90 and 263-264.
% c171 (melody; A&S Unit 3, PDF 50 sec. 3: high notes and dissonant notes attract accent; PDF 52 sec. 7: rhythmic groups; PDF 54 sec. 9: the appoggiatura is an accented dissonance): c131 restored the solo's A4-G4-F4 at 117 (the appoggiatura of F2's third bar), so Vln I's first answer, written by c063 for the old held G4, ran F6-A5 in eighths over the solo's peak and its resolution. The answer moves to 119 beats 2-4, over the solo's held C4 (the consequent's first note, as 122 answers the held A3): G6-F6-E flat 6-D6-C6-A5 down the iio7 (A-C-E flat-G), landing on the leading tone F sharp 5 at 120 (V), as the 122 answer ends G5-F sharp 5 before its G (a B flat 5-A5 ending made fifths with the solo's E flat 4-D4). Notes of the solo unchanged.
% c174 (orchestration; Casanelles ch. 6, PDF 286: a section sounds fuller and brighter than one player; PDF 287: an instrument alone in its frequency range is clear and present; PDF 271-273: the printed level is the level wanted in the result): 119 and 122 Vln I's answers pp (were p, the solo's own level):
% a whole section in G6-F sharp 5, two octaves above the single bassoon and above everything but Vln II's pizz. G5, carries at pp and answers the solo without outweighing it.
% c209 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 58-59 and the note to Ex. 24, PDF 200: a melody in the violins' very high register is isolated unless doubled an octave below; one or two desks keep the upper octave and the rest of Vln I play the octave below, so the piercing top is softened and the line gains a clearer sound; Ex. 26, PDF 202-203, and Ex. 36, PDF 214: two solo violins on top, the rest of Vln I an octave lower, the solo desk prominent enough because the dynamic is pp):
% 119 and 122 Vln I's pp answers to the bassoon solo (G6-A5 | F sharp5; D6-F sharp5 | G5) were the whole section alone at the top of the E string, three octaves above the solo. Now Vln I divide: 2 soli keep the line at pitch (upper note) and the rest double it an octave lower (octave chords, as c109 wrote 159-164) (G5-A4 | F sharp4; D5-F sharp4 | G4). Top line, bassoon solo and all other parts unchanged.
% c300 (MIDI dynamics; Rimsky PDF 128, imitation and echo: the answer is weaker than the phrase it answers): the two solo desks' upper voice at 119 and 122 had no dynamic of its own, so the MIDI and the audio render played the G6 and D6 answers at the default velocity (90) over the section's pp (41); the upper voice now continues the main voice (\voiceOne, the section in a \new Voice \voiceTwo) and takes a hidden pp on the rest at 119.1, so both answers play pp; the printed pp and markups are unchanged.
% c318 (expression; Adler ch. 2, PDF 38, 42-43, 45, Ex. II-20 and II-29; martele at the frog): down-bows on the sf chords at 101 and 105 (Vla, Vc, Cb), which followed the staccato quarter of F1 on an up-bow; 105 Vln I-II both accented quarters down-bow, as at 90; 110 Vln II and Vla down-bow on the ff climax, as Vln I, Vc and Cb already were.
% c339 (Rimsky-Korsakov, Principles, PDF 129-130, Ex. 254 (Tsar's Bride 142, PDF 418): a note is stressed by 'a chain of three or four grace notes in the form of a scale', an upward glide slurred to the main note, not to a chord in the strings): 110 Vln I D6-E6-F6 and Vln II D5-E5-F5 glide into the ff G6/G5 where the brass enter on the lament's goal; bowing and the down-bow unchanged.
% c355 (Adler ch. 2, PDF 37-39: a slur is one bow; about six notes to a bow at f, Ex. II-22; the accented goal takes a new bow, as this score's 9-10): 131-132 Vln I-II the slur of the run ends on its sixth eighth, so the accented G-major cadence note at 132.1 (Vln I G6, Vln II D5) is a fresh down-bow (the run is an anacrusis, up-bow) and Vln II no longer slurs D5 into D5.
devVlnOne = { R1*4 | r2 r4 f'''8(\mf es'''8 | des'''8 c''' bes'' a'' \once \offset positions #'(-0.25 . 0.25) Beam bes'' c''' des''' es''') | f'''4->\downbow f''4->\downbow r2 | R1 | c'''1~\p\< | c'''2 c'''4--( bes''4) | b''2 c'''4( g''4) | \grace { d'''16( e''' f''' } g'''1)\ff~ | g'''4( aes'''2 f'''4) | f'''2~( f'''4 e'''4) | f'''2-> r2 | fis''1\p\> | R1*2\! | R1*2 | r4-\tweak stencil ##f \pp << { \voiceOne g'''8(^\markup \italic "dolce, 2 soli" f''' es''' d''' c''' a'' | fis''4) } \new Voice { \voiceTwo g''8(\pp_\markup \italic "gli altri" f'' es'' d'' c'' a' | fis'4) } >> \oneVoice r4 r2 | R1 | r4 << { \voiceOne d'''8(^\markup \italic "2 soli" c''' b'' a'' g'' fis'' | g''4) } \new Voice { \voiceTwo d''8(\pp_\markup \italic "gli altri" c'' b' a' g' fis' | g'4) } >> \oneVoice r4 r2 | R1*4 | r2 r4 a''4(\mf\<_\markup \italic "unis." | b''2 g''2) | a''1 | r4 a''8(\f b'' \once \offset positions #'(-0.25 . 0.25) Beam c''' d''' e''' fis''') | g'''4-> d'''4-> b''4-> g''4-> | g''1\>~ | g''2.\p r4 | }
% c027 (texture; Adler II PDF 264, 267, 274): 115-122 bassoon solo: clarinets tacet (no same-family sustained notes in the solo's register); Vln II pizz. an octave higher (C5-G5; 119 C-E flat, so the top does not move A-D in octaves with the bass into 120), above the solo.
% c063: 119 beat 4 Vln II A4-C5 (was C5-E flat5): its E flat5 moved in octaves with the bassoon solo's E flat4-D4 into 120 (E flat5-D5 on beat 2), heard once the violas' pizz. left the solo's register.
% c067 (texture; Sebesky ch. 3, PDF 140 rule 6 and PDF 142 Ex. 202: the lead line reinforced an octave lower at the climax of a build): 128-130 Vln II leaves Hn 1's G4/F sharp4 pad and plays the tune an octave below Vln I (A4 | B4 G4 | A4), the last step of the scoring crescendo into 131.
% c092 (A&S PDF 76: the 3rd of a triad is never omitted except for special effects; PDF 89 point 4): 134 Vln II B4 (was D5 held), so the G7 keeps its 3rd after Hn 3-4 leave at 133 (G-B-F-G, the 5th omitted as root-position sevenths allow).
% c093 (A&S Unit 14, PDF 214, 228): 116 Vln II pizz. D5-A5 (was D5-F sharp5 over the bass F sharp: the leading tone doubled and both rose to G a beat apart).
% c132: 101 and 105 Cl 1's mf is now the printed one (Cl 2 enters with it on beat 2); Cl 2 carries a hidden mf on its beat-1 rest for the MIDI and prints its own mf in its part.
% c132 (harmony; A&S Unit 23, PDF 394-395, cross relation; PDF 376-377, a dissonance does not sound against its own resolution): 117 the violas rest on beat 2 (their G4-B flat4 pizz. lay around the restored solo A4, with its resolution G4 at the same pitch; Vln II's E flat5-G5 carries the beat); 117 Vln I's run ends B flat5-A5 (was A flat5, a cross relation with the solo's A natural 4 in the same bar); 119 beat 4 Vln II G5 alone (c112's E flat5 there moved in octaves with the solo's E flat4-D4 into 120, the doubling c063 had removed). 101 and 105 Hn 1-2 and Cl 2 enter on beat 2 with Bn 1 (on beat 1 their E-G and A-C sounded against the strings' sf F and B flat chords, a major 7th with the bass that did not resolve; PDF 377 box).
% c152 (harmony; A&S Unit 29, PDF 504 Ex. 29-3: the augmented sixth is the Phrygian cadence's flat 6 with sharp 4 added; PDF 505: the Italian sixth, third doubled, suits a thin texture and goes straight to V; PDF 510: in minor it follows I directly):
%   121 beats 3-4 the bass (Vc, Cb pizz.) takes E flat under the solo's G3 and Vln II's C sharp 5 / G5 with the violas' G4 make an Italian sixth E flat-G-C sharp, which opens to the octave D at 122 (C sharp-D, E flat-D, G-F sharp); 122 violas A4-D5 (was A4-C5: C5 a beat after Vln II's C sharp 5 in the same octave was a cross relation), so the Italian sixth opens to the plain V, as A&S Ex. 29-2 and 29-7 show; the G-minor half cadence now has the flat 6 that the exposition's Phrygian half cadences have at 57-58 and 177-178.
% c233 (form; A&S Unit 18, PDF 298-299, Ex. 18-12: a passing chord over 7 leads i to the iv6 of a Phrygian cadence, 'a completely stepwise bass motion through the descending 4th 1-5'): 121 beat 2 cellos and basses pizz. F (passing i4/2 under the upper strings' G-B flat-D, which the solo's held B flat3 requires instead of v6), so the G-minor half cadence has the bass G-F-E flat-D, the diatonic form of the development's own lament bass (107-110).
% c276 (playability; Adler ch. 2, adler1 PDF 30: without "div." a double note is played as a double stop, and "unis." must follow a divisi; PDF 41, Ex. II-59: a plucked
%   chord is arpeggiated from the bottom unless the composer asks otherwise): Vln II's pp pizz. thirds and sixths are divided like the violas' ("pizz. div."), so each beat is
%   one clean pluck under the solo, not a rolled double stop; "unis." where they take the bow again (67 / 127). Notes unchanged.
% c352 (A&S Unit 27, PDF 463-464 Ex. 27-1, 27-2, 27-4a; 467 sec. 4; 470 Ex. 27-11): 120 beat 4, the pizzicato off-beat chord over V7 of G minor becomes V minor-ninth (F sharp-A-C-E flat over the D bass of 120.1):
%   Vln II E flat5 (5-flat6-5 neighbour, D5-E flat5-D5) and Vla C5 (7th); the 9th and 7th fall in parallel thirds to D5/B flat4 at 121.2. Bassoon solo, Vln I and bass unchanged.
