% c385 (Gould PDF 51-52): 124 the violas' rest before the sul D entry is r2 r4 (half-bar shown), as at the score's other 2/2 entries on beat 4.
% c418 (Adler PDF 38, 42: strong attacks on a down-bow; mark only against the players' habit): down-bows on the accented arrivals 113.1 (violas, basses; the cellos and violins arrive down-bow already) and 132.1 (violas, cellos, basses; the violins arrive down-bow already). Signs only.
devVla = { f'2->(\f c'4. des'8 | es'4-.) des'8( c'8 bes4-.) aes4-. | <aes c'>4-.\sf\downbow r4 r2 | R1 | bes'2->(\f f'4. ges'8 | aes'4-.) ges'8( f'8 es'4-.) des'4-. | <des' f'>4-.\sf\downbow r4 r2 | R1 | c'2\p\< c'2 | c'2 bes2 | aes2 aes4 g4 | g4\ff\downbow c'8( bes aes g f g) | f8( g aes bes aes2) | g2 c'8( bes aes g) | aes2->\downbow r2 | a2.\p\> r4\! | r4 <g' bes'>4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz. div." r4 <g' bes'>4 | r4 <a' c''>4 r4 <g' bes'>4 | r2 r4 <g' bes'>4 | r4 <g' c''>4 r4 <g' bes'>4 | r4 <a' c''>4 r4 <a' c''>4 | r4 <a' d''>4 r4 <a' c''>4 | r4 <g' bes'>4 r4 g'4 | r4 <a' d''>4 r2 | r1 | r2 r4 \once \override TextScript.avoid-slur = #'outside \once \override TextScript.outside-staff-priority = #600 \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.text = \markup \italic "sul D" d'4(\p^\markup \italic "arco, unis."\startTextSpan | a'2 g'4 fis'4 | e'2 d'2)\stopTextSpan | e'1\pp | c'1 | b2\< r2 | d'2->(\mf\< a4. b8 | c'4-.\f) b8( a8 g4-.) fis4-. | <d' b'>4->\downbow r4 r2 | d'1\>~ | d'2.\p r4 | }
% c211 (melody; Adler ch. 3, adler1 PDF 99: the cello's D string 'is the most musically captivating string... warm and lyrical', the A string 'the most brilliant and piercing'; PDF 73-74: an open string stands out among stopped notes in an expressive line; PDF 73: timbre is the reason to name a string): 123-124 the cellos' F2 entry D4-B3-A3-G3-B3-D4, p espress., is marked 'sul D' (dashed line to its last note), so the hand-over's first statement is on the warm D string with no open A3 on 124's downbeat; notes, dynamics and the hand-over order unchanged.
% c292 (harmony; A&S Unit 13, PDF 201-202 sec. 5, Ex. 13-11a/b (Beethoven, Waldstein): after V7 a substitute for the root-position tonic 'prevents a cadential effect too early in the passage';
%   PDF 167 Ex. 11-7 and PDF 202 Ex. 13-12: V-VI risks parallel 5ths; PDF 182 Ex. 12-8 and PDF 184 Ex. 12-11: cadential 6/4 before V, its 4th may rise to the 5th of V when another voice falls):
%   128-129 and 131-132 were two root-position V7-I cadences in G four bars apart, so the first took the weight of the development's goal at 132. VI is ruled out (the tune's A5-B5 over D-E
%   would be parallel 5ths), so 129 is I6 - I6/4: Vc B2-D3, Cb B1-D2 (were G3/G2 whole notes), harp LH B2-D3-G3-B3-D3-G3-B3-D4, Hn 4 G3-G3-B3-B3 (was B3 x4, which tripled the bass B);
%   the 7th C4 (Vla, Bn 1) still falls to B3. The D bass then holds from 129.3 to 131 (I6/4 - V - V7), so 131-132 is the G-major area's only authentic cadence; Hn 1 G4-F#4 and Hn 4 B3-A3
%   resolve the 6/4 at 130 while the tune's G5 rises to A5. Tune, hand-over and dynamics unchanged.
devVc = { f2->(\f c4. des8 | es4-.) des8( c8 bes,4-.) aes,4-. | <f c'>4-.\sf\downbow r4 r2 | R1 | bes2->(\f f4. ges8 | aes4-.) ges8( f8 es4-.) des4-. | <bes f'>4-.\sf\downbow r4 r2 | R1 | f2\p\< e2 | es2 d2 | des2 c2 | c'4\ff c'8( bes aes g f g) | f2 des2 | bes,2 c8( bes, aes, g,) | f,2-> r2 | d2.\p\>\upbow r4\! | g4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz." r4 r2 | fis4 r4 g4 r4 | es4 r4 r2 | c4 r4 bes4 r4 | a4 r4 r2 | d4 r4 r2 | g4 f4 es4 r4 | d4 r4 r2 | \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.text = \markup \italic "sul D" d'2.(\p^\markup \italic "arco, espress."\startTextSpan b4 | a4 g4 b4 d'4)\stopTextSpan | e1\pp | c2 b,2 | a,1 | d1 | b,2\< e2 | d1 | d2\f d2 | g,4->\downbow r4 r2 | f1\>~ | f2.\p r4 | }
% c239: Cb 104.4 D-flat held full value (no staccato) so the bass sounds under the Cl 1 F5 pedal to the end of the theme.
devCb = { f,2->(\f c,4. des,8 | es,4-.) des,8( c,8 bes,,4-.) aes,,4-. | f,4-.\sf\downbow r4 r2 | R1 | bes,2->(\f f,4. ges,8 | aes,4-.) ges,8( f,8 es,4-.) des,4 | bes,,4-.\sf\downbow r4 r2 | R1 | f,2\p\< e,2 | es,2 d,2 | des,2 c,2 | c,1\ff | f,2 des,2 | bes,,2 c,2 | f,,2->\downbow r2 | d,2.\p\>\upbow r4\! | g,4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz." r4 r2 | fis,4 r4 g,4 r4 | es,4 r4 r2 | c,4 r4 bes,4 r4 | a,4 r4 r2 | d,4 r4 r2 | g,4 f,4 es,4 r4 | d,4 r4 r2 | g,1\pp^\markup \italic "arco" | fis,2 g,2 | e,1 | c,2 b,,2 | a,,1 | d,1 | b,,2\< e,2 | d,1 | d,2\f d,2 | g,,4->\downbow r4 r2 | f,1\>~ | f,2.\p r4 | }
% c053 (form; A&S Unit 18, PDF 291-292: in a series of 6/3 chords only the beginning and the end are points of articulation, and the end matters for its cadential
% function; Ex. 18-8a, 18-12, PDF 295, 298-299: the 6/3 descent with 7-6 suspensions leads to the cadence): the lament's 6/3 descent (107-109) arrives on V at 110 and the
% F-minor cadence 110-113 is the development's goal. It was f with no trumpets or tuba, so the development's loudest point was below the exposition (27, 91 ff) and the
% retransition (147 ff). Dvorak 9/iv's development peaks ff with the trumpets (177-191) before the recapitulation. 110-113 now ff, trumpets 1-3 and tuba join
% (Tpt 1 on Ob 1's E5-F5 then Fl 1's F5-E5-F5, Tpt 2 = Tbn 1 an octave up and Vln II, Tpt 3 = Hn 1, tuba = basses), timpani C and A flat at 111-112.
% c097 (texture; Adler ch. 11, PDF 44-47, Bartok Concerto for Orchestra i 313-386: the fugato passes from strings to the brass, 'f, ben marc.'; two horns in unison = one trombone): 103-104 Hn 3-4 (a2, with the violas) and Tbn 1 (with the cellos) take the B flat minor head instead of a single punctuation chord, so the second statement is louder and brighter than the first (99, strings alone).
% c098 (expression; A&S Unit 21, PDF 348 sec. 4: dissonant suspensions 'receive something of an accent and their resolutions are softer'; PDF 350 sec. 8: the cadential 4-3 over V is the
% suspension's most important use; Dvorak 9/iv 176, 182, 190: trumpets and trombones ff with the strings at the development's climaxes): 110-113 horns, trumpets, trombones and tuba ff (were f since c054),
% so the climax's 9-10 (111) and cadential 4-3 (112, Tpt 1 and Hn 2 F-E with the violins) are no longer a level below the exposition codetta (91) and the retransition (147), both ff in full brass.
% Answer to c054: the melody's top octave (Vln I, Fl 1, G6-A flat6-F6-E6-F6) lies more than an octave above the highest brass note (Tpt 1 F5), and Tpt 1 itself doubles the G5 octave's F5-E5-F5. Timpani stay f (c056).
% c098: 105 Hn 3-4 and Tbn 1, which double the B flat minor head since c097, end it with the strings' sf. 108 Vln I's restruck 7th C6 over D (the 7-6) takes a tenuto (PDF 347: restruck suspension; PDF 348 sec. 4).
devTptOne = { R1*11 | e''1\ff | f''1 | f''2~( f''4 e''4) | f''2-> r2 | R1*21 | }
devTptTwo = { R1*11 | c''1\ff | c''2 des''2~ | des''2( c''2) | c''2-> r2 | R1*21 | }
% c060 (cyclic lens; Sebesky ch. 1, PDF 11: a colour saved for its entrance keeps its surprise; arrival.ily: 223 is the first sustained forte C-major chord in full brass):
% 110 and 112 (second half) Tpt 3 holds B flat 4 (was G4 = Hn 1), so the brass sound V7 of F, not a root-position C-major triad, and the 7th falls to A flat 4 at 111 and 113.
devTptThree = { R1*11 | bes'1\ff | aes'2 aes'2 | bes'1 | aes'2-> r2 | R1*21 | }
devTuba = { R1*11 | c,1\ff | f,,2 des,2 | bes,,2 c,2 | f,,2-> r2 | R1*21 | }
